WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:06.506 align:start position:0% line:0%
In a building over there,
is a camera that absolutely
blows my mind.

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:09.642 align:start position:0% line:0%
Is this camera capable
of filming the speed of light?

00:00:09.675 --> 00:00:12.311 align:start size:69% position:31%
- Yeah.
- Dan: Heck.

00:00:12.344 --> 00:00:15.782
- Very high tech stuff, guys.
- The maximum speed we can do

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:17.483 align:start size:63% position:38%
is 10 trillion
frames per second.

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:19.786 align:start position:0%
This is the reason we came here.
Crank it up all the way?

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:21.421 align:start size:69% position:31%
- Yeah.
- All right.

00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:25.025 size:84% position:42%
I just feel like no human
should ever have seen this.

00:00:29.695 --> 00:00:32.498 align:start position:0% line:0%
We've filmed
the fastest thing.

00:00:32.531 --> 00:00:33.800 align:start position:0% line:0%
We've hit our ceiling.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:35.334 align:start size:63% position:38% line:0%
It's not going to be
anytime soon

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:37.236 align:start size:75% position:25%
that we're going to film
anything faster, so...

00:00:37.269 --> 00:00:39.505
Not this year, maybe.

00:00:39.538 --> 00:00:40.640
Should we just invent
some stuff that's faster?

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:42.341
Let's just start
making up stuff.

00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:44.177 align:start size:66% position:34%
Yeah, this camera is,
like, 50 trillion--

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.545
You clearly haven't seen
super light.

00:00:47.713 --> 00:00:49.816
It was really interesting,
the set-up.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:52.485
You can tell that so many
thousands of hours of research

00:00:52.518 --> 00:00:54.487
had gone into creating
that camera.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.390 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, the footage,
you can't really tell
what's happening.

00:00:57.423 --> 00:00:59.625
When you think about
what's actually happening,

00:00:59.658 --> 00:01:02.195
what you're actually seeing,
it's more impressive than
the actual footage.

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:04.363 align:start position:0%
Yeah,
it's one of those things
where the idea of it

00:01:04.396 --> 00:01:06.365 align:start position:0%
is way more impressive
than the visual of it.

00:01:06.398 --> 00:01:07.834
'Cause the visual of it
looks like

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.436
a slightly outdated
film effect.

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:13.406
With a picture
of a bottle on it.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:15.575 line:0%
<i> ( synth music playing )</i>

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.415
I would say of all
the videos we've shot,

00:01:22.448 --> 00:01:24.383
this one,
while we were shooting it,

00:01:24.416 --> 00:01:25.852
I was struggling to comprehend

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:27.854
how the camera was actually
acquiring those images.

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:30.857
And also then,
you've got to think about
the time scale involved.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:35.728
Like, I can't get my head around
how short a picosecond is.

00:01:35.761 --> 00:01:39.532 align:start size:94% position:6%
- It's absolutely insane.
- Well, nothing else happens
  on that sort of scale.

00:01:39.565 --> 00:01:41.868 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well, as we're struggling
to wrap our heads around it,

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:43.369 align:start size:84% position:16%
we thought it would be best
to actually ask

00:01:43.402 --> 00:01:46.239 align:start size:88% position:13%
one of the people
who invented the technology.

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:48.741 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
We're here with Lihong,
who's one of the inventors
of the camera.

00:01:48.774 --> 00:01:51.410 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
Do you think you
could talk us through it,
exactly how it works,

00:01:51.443 --> 00:01:53.446 align:start size:56% position:44%
and we can try
and understand it,

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:55.414 align:start size:72% position:28%
because I just
don't get it right now.

00:01:55.447 --> 00:01:57.683 align:start position:0%
Sure. For the first time,
humans can see

00:01:57.716 --> 00:01:59.552 align:start position:0%
how a light pulse
propagates in space.

00:01:59.585 --> 00:02:01.587 align:start position:0%
But before
I talk about our camera,

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.756 align:start position:0% line:0%
let me talk about
the standard streak camera.

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:06.259 line:0%
<i> A standard streak camera
 gives you a 1-D movie</i>

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:07.560
<i> that's really fast.</i>

00:02:07.593 --> 00:02:09.228
<i> You only allow
 the X dimension,</i>

00:02:09.261 --> 00:02:11.430
<i> or horizontal dimension,
 to pass through,</i>

00:02:11.463 --> 00:02:14.634
<i> then you watch a horse race
 through a slit.</i>

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.269 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It won't be as fun.
- Right.

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:18.271 align:start position:0%
We're so used to
your smartphone cameras

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:21.340 align:start position:0%
seeing at least the world
in 2-D, if not 3-D.

00:02:21.373 --> 00:02:22.909 size:81% position:41%
Yeah, it's a world
you can relate to.

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:25.811 align:start position:0%
Exactly.
And so let me talk about
how our camera works.

00:02:25.844 --> 00:02:28.481
<i> Let's say we start
 with this object.</i>

00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:30.550
<i> But before we send it
 to a standard streak camera,</i>

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:31.884 line:0%
<i> we're going to route it</i>

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:34.453 line:0%
<i> to this digital
 micromirror device.</i>

00:02:34.486 --> 00:02:37.390
<i> We write this code,
 and that's an important step,</i>

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:39.792
<i> because that allows us
 later on to decode everything</i>

00:02:39.825 --> 00:02:41.627
<i> to generate a 2-D movie.</i>

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.629 line:0%
<i> And this encoded object</i>

00:02:43.662 --> 00:02:46.465 line:0%
<i> is routed
 to this streak camera</i>

00:02:46.498 --> 00:02:48.935 line:0%
<i> Unlike the standard use
 of the streak camera,</i>

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:50.570 line:0%
<i> we allow the wide dimension,</i>

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:52.305 line:0%
<i> or the vertical dimension
 to come in.</i>

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.640 line:0%
<i> At the back end,
 you can use a slower camera</i>

00:02:54.673 --> 00:02:57.810 line:0%
<i> to capture whatever
 you want to detect.</i>

00:02:57.843 --> 00:02:59.312
<i> It's a great invention.</i>

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:00.646
<i> This technology allows us</i>

00:03:00.679 --> 00:03:02.481
<i> to look at really short
 time intervals.</i>

00:03:02.514 --> 00:03:04.350 align:start size:53% position:47%
Why do we have to
use a laser

00:03:04.383 --> 00:03:06.719 align:start size:59% position:41%
instead of just
a normal light bulb
or a flashlight?

00:03:06.752 --> 00:03:10.489 align:start position:0%
A flashlight
turns on too slowly
for us to observe.

00:03:10.522 --> 00:03:12.825 align:start position:0%
A laser,
we can make it really short.

00:03:12.858 --> 00:03:15.828 align:start position:0%
So we use a state of the art
laser pulse,

00:03:15.861 --> 00:03:19.298 align:start position:0%
and that turns on within
a femtosecond scale.

00:03:19.331 --> 00:03:21.334 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, the laser pulse
is on the femto scale?

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:24.503 align:start position:0%
Right, right. Yeah.
But our camera works so fast...

00:03:24.536 --> 00:03:26.973 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, I was going to say.
- ...even nanoseconds
  feels like eternity.

00:03:27.006 --> 00:03:29.575 align:start size:91% position:9%
This camera makes it look
like the light's just
hanging out forever,

00:03:29.608 --> 00:03:32.345
<i> but it's really still
 on the pico scale.</i>

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:33.779
Lihong:<i> Right,
 you're literally watching</i>

00:03:33.812 --> 00:03:36.515
<i> the slowest slow motion
 movies right now.</i>

00:03:36.548 --> 00:03:40.920 align:start position:0%
The hope is that our camera
can be used to study
some fundamental physics.

00:03:40.953 --> 00:03:43.522 size:78% position:39%
We've all heard of sonar,
but there's an optical

00:03:43.555 --> 00:03:45.791 align:start position:0%
counterpart of sonar
called lidar.

00:03:45.824 --> 00:03:47.860 line:0%
Very much like radar, as well.

00:03:47.893 --> 00:03:50.997 align:start position:0% line:0%
You basically detect
a round trip time.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.366 align:start position:0% line:0%
So, from the time,
you can measure distance.

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:57.003 align:start size:69% position:31%
Could that be used
to see around corners?

00:03:57.036 --> 00:03:59.872 align:start position:0%
Yes, it is feasible,
although we haven't
done that physically.

00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:01.974 align:start size:94% position:6%
- That's like a superpower.
- Yeah.

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:03.876 align:start position:0%
For now, we're doing something
very similar to that.

00:04:03.909 --> 00:04:07.546 align:start position:0%
Let's say you have
a foggy situation
or a cloudy situation.

00:04:07.579 --> 00:04:09.015
<i> To your naked eye,</i>

00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:10.983
<i> you would not see
 the airplane at all.</i>

00:04:11.016 --> 00:04:13.552
<i> So we can just
 see through the cloud</i>

00:04:13.585 --> 00:04:14.954
<i> and get a movie like this.</i>

00:04:14.987 --> 00:04:17.023 align:start position:0% line:0%
You can see
this little airplane.

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:18.691 line:0%
Gav:<i> And I'm seeing
 you can also see</i>

00:04:18.724 --> 00:04:19.925 line:0%
<i> the depth information
 this way, as well.</i>

00:04:19.958 --> 00:04:21.861 line:0%
Lihong:
<i> You get depth information.</i>

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:23.729 line:0%
<i> This would be actually much
 more accurate than our eye.</i>

00:04:23.762 --> 00:04:25.398 size:78% position:39%
And this could be useful,

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:28.734 align:start position:0%
even maybe in the future
for autonomous cars.

00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:31.404 align:start position:0%
We want to drive
in a cloudy situation--

00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:33.406 align:start position:0%
a foggy situation,

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:35.474 align:start position:0%
and you can see objects
through the fog.

00:04:35.507 --> 00:04:38.411 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Another superpower.
- Yeah.

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:41.047 size:81% position:41%
We're also thinking about
something more biological.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:45.918 align:start position:0%
We know that our brain
is the most complex machine
probably in the universe.

00:04:45.951 --> 00:04:49.755 align:start position:0%
I want to figure out
how our brain computes
one plus one, even.

00:04:49.788 --> 00:04:52.391 align:start position:0%
By using our camera,
we can potentially see that.

00:04:52.424 --> 00:04:54.927 align:start size:88% position:13%
Would this be useful
for detecting damage
in the brain?

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:56.429 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like, if someone's
had a stroke,

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:57.630 align:start size:88% position:13%
would you be able
to see that on this?

00:04:57.663 --> 00:04:58.898 size:81% position:41%
That's certainly our hope.

00:04:58.931 --> 00:05:00.433 align:start size:59% position:41%
Does that mean
that in the future,

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:02.335 align:start size:59% position:41%
people will be able
to read my mind?

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:04.003 align:start position:0%
That's one possibility.

00:05:04.036 --> 00:05:07.440 align:start size:88% position:13%
I just feel like
it must feel like
such an accomplishment

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:11.610 align:start size:88% position:13%
to have seen
the fastest thing.

00:05:11.643 --> 00:05:17.450 align:start position:0%
It was a surreal moment
when we got the first movie
at light speed.

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:19.952 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because that's
the ceiling as we know it.

00:05:19.985 --> 00:05:22.688 align:start position:0%
We never thought
it would be possible, yes.

00:05:22.721 --> 00:05:24.090 align:start size:81% position:19%
Well, thank you very much,
Lihong,

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:26.726 align:start size:84% position:16%
for teaching us
all this fascinating stuff.

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:28.561 size:84% position:58%
I'm going to be thinking
about this on the way home.

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:30.496
I'm going to be watching this
and trying to understand it.

00:05:30.529 --> 00:05:32.898 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Several times.
- All right, back to you,
  Gav and Dan.

00:05:32.931 --> 00:05:34.133 align:start size:44% position:56%
Thanks, us.

00:05:34.166 --> 00:05:36.469 align:start size:88% position:13%
This is our
time cheat sheet,

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:39.572 align:start size:88% position:13%
just a list of all
the different time scales.

00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:42.808 align:start size:88% position:13%
We usually hang around
milliseconds.

00:05:42.841 --> 00:05:44.810 align:start size:88% position:13%
A lot of our exposures
are down to the microsecond.

00:05:44.843 --> 00:05:47.646 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah.
- Sometimes when
  we're using the 2511,

00:05:47.679 --> 00:05:50.649 align:start size:94% position:6%
we'll have exposure times
that are pushing on
the hundredths of nanoseconds,

00:05:50.682 --> 00:05:52.818 align:start size:88% position:13%
but that's about as far
as our Phantoms go.

00:05:52.851 --> 00:05:55.988 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah.
- We were down here
  with this camera

00:05:56.021 --> 00:05:58.424 align:start size:88% position:13%
and around
in this ballpark.

00:05:58.457 --> 00:06:01.060 align:start position:0%
But before, right,
I kind of had it
all comprehended.

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:03.596 align:start position:0%
I had it all in my mind,
like, "Yeah, this is
the amount of time."

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:04.997 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Now I've lost it again.
- Well, what--

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.032
- That many zeroes?
- What blows my mind

00:06:07.065 --> 00:06:10.469 align:start size:88% position:13%
is we had
femtosecond long
piece of light

00:06:10.502 --> 00:06:12.004 align:start size:84% position:16%
- going all around...
- Yeah, we did.

00:06:12.037 --> 00:06:15.941 align:start size:88% position:13%
...filming it with
picosecond differences
between frames.

00:06:15.974 --> 00:06:19.512 align:start size:88% position:13%
What on Earth
is all this about?

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:22.014 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yoctosecond?

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:25.818 align:start size:88% position:13%
Surely, that would make
the light look like
you blinking.

00:06:25.851 --> 00:06:27.820
<i> At this point,
 I feel like you might</i>

00:06:27.853 --> 00:06:30.823
<i> as well just say infinity.</i>

00:06:30.856 --> 00:06:32.691 align:start size:81% position:19%
We've actually
had a lot of comments over
the years of doing this

00:06:32.724 --> 00:06:35.528 align:start size:72% position:28%
asking us to film light
with our Phantom.

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:37.596 align:start size:56% position:44%
Like, film a torch
or a laser beam

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:38.731 align:start size:47% position:53%
or a light bulb
or something.

00:06:38.764 --> 00:06:40.032 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I always
have to explain to them,

00:06:40.065 --> 00:06:42.868 align:start size:88% position:13%
light is so fast
that our Phantom camera

00:06:42.901 --> 00:06:45.938 align:start size:88% position:13%
sees light
the exact same way
as our eyes do.

00:06:45.971 --> 00:06:48.207 size:84% position:58%
So just for the hell of it,

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:49.642 align:start size:63% position:38%
thought I'd
demonstrate it here.

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:52.812 align:start size:88% position:13%
Here's the light.
Here's a piece of paper.

00:06:52.845 --> 00:06:55.548 align:start size:88% position:13%
Let's play it.

00:06:55.581 --> 00:06:58.451
<i> Boop. All right.
 There it is, instantly on.</i>

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:00.553
<i> I just dropped that to prove
 that it was in slow-mo.</i>

00:07:00.586 --> 00:07:02.688 align:start position:0%
-<i> Because otherwise-- </i>
- Dan:<i> They wouldn't believe us.</i>

00:07:02.721 --> 00:07:04.623
Gav:<i> They wouldn't
 believe us otherwise.</i>

00:07:04.656 --> 00:07:06.559 align:start size:88% position:13%
I thought a good way
to demonstrate just how fast

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:09.728 size:84% position:42%
100 billion plus
frames a second is,

00:07:09.761 --> 00:07:11.564 align:start size:88% position:13%
we could just look
at some of our old footage

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:12.898 align:start size:88% position:13%
and determine how long
it would have taken

00:07:12.931 --> 00:07:14.733 align:start size:88% position:13%
at 100 billion
frames a second,

00:07:14.766 --> 00:07:16.068 align:start size:88% position:13%
or 10 trillion.

00:07:16.101 --> 00:07:17.736 align:start size:56% position:44%
All right, let me
pull something up.

00:07:17.769 --> 00:07:19.705 line:0%
<i> Lightning strike.</i>

00:07:19.738 --> 00:07:20.906
One of the fastest things

00:07:20.939 --> 00:07:22.208 size:88% position:56%
we've ever filmed previously

00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:24.543 align:start size:88% position:13%
before we immediately
beat it.

00:07:24.576 --> 00:07:27.513 size:88% position:56%
If we were to film this at
100 billion frames a second.

00:07:27.546 --> 00:07:30.683 align:start size:88% position:13%
to watch the whole clip
played back at 24

00:07:30.716 --> 00:07:32.751 align:start size:88% position:13%
would take almost
four months.

00:07:32.784 --> 00:07:33.886 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Four months?
- Yeah.

00:07:33.919 --> 00:07:34.954 align:start position:0% line:0%
And it's just
happening right there.

00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:36.021 line:0%
Gav:
<i> And there it is.</i>

00:07:36.054 --> 00:07:37.490 line:0%
<i> And if somehow we were able</i>

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:38.757 align:start size:88% position:13%
to film this at
10 trillion frames a second,

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:40.759 align:start size:88% position:13%
the whole strike
from beginning to end

00:07:40.792 --> 00:07:43.062
would take 32 years.

00:07:43.095 --> 00:07:45.498 align:start position:0%
Imagine the length
of that storm.

00:07:45.531 --> 00:07:48.100 align:start size:88% position:13%
I can actually
simulate exactly what
that would look like

00:07:48.133 --> 00:07:50.069
if filmed at 10 trillion.

00:07:50.102 --> 00:07:52.938
Here we go,
and it's playing now.

00:07:52.971 --> 00:07:54.940 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's exactly
what it would look like.

00:07:54.973 --> 00:07:57.576 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Flippin'-A.
- All right, another clip?

00:07:57.609 --> 00:07:58.944 align:start position:0%
No, no.
Watch the whole thing.

00:07:58.977 --> 00:08:00.012 align:start size:22% position:78%
Okay.

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:04.950 align:start size:84% position:16%
- 32 years.
- That should take us
  into retirement.

00:08:04.983 --> 00:08:06.986 align:start position:0%
Yeah, I think so.

00:08:07.019 --> 00:08:08.120
( no audible dialogue )

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:17.296 align:start size:88% position:13%
Next clip.
Remember this bad lad?

00:08:17.329 --> 00:08:19.632 line:0%
Dan:
<i> I do. Sherman round.</i>

00:08:19.665 --> 00:08:21.901 line:0%
Gav:<i> We filmed this
 at 12,000 frames a second.</i>

00:08:21.934 --> 00:08:22.968 line:0%
<i> There it is
 hurtling through the air.</i>

00:08:23.001 --> 00:08:24.303 line:0%
<i> If we'd have filmed this</i>

00:08:24.336 --> 00:08:25.671 line:0%
<i> at 100 billion frames
 a second,</i>

00:08:25.704 --> 00:08:27.973 align:start size:88% position:13%
from the muzzle
to when it goes out

00:08:28.006 --> 00:08:30.109 align:start size:88% position:13%
of the end of frame
would take eight years.

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:32.278 align:start position:0%
Well, that would
kind of defeat the object

00:08:32.311 --> 00:08:33.979 align:start position:0%
of having a tank
firing at something.

00:08:34.012 --> 00:08:36.815 align:start position:0%
They'd just be, like,

00:08:36.848 --> 00:08:38.284 align:start position:0%
"I guess I'll move
out of the way."

00:08:38.317 --> 00:08:39.985 align:start size:69% position:31%
You could serve
two presidential terms

00:08:40.018 --> 00:08:41.587 size:88% position:56%
before it went out of frame.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:43.656 line:0%
<i> Now just for giggles,</i>

00:08:43.689 --> 00:08:44.823 line:0%
<i> it's pretty easy math--</i>

00:08:44.856 --> 00:08:45.925 line:0%
<i> you just add two zeroes.</i>

00:08:45.958 --> 00:08:47.660 align:start size:88% position:13%
If we filmed it
at 10 trillion,

00:08:47.693 --> 00:08:49.194 align:start size:88% position:13%
800 years from
beginning to end.

00:08:49.227 --> 00:08:53.232 align:start position:0%
So, like, quite a few
Queen Elizabeths.

00:08:53.265 --> 00:08:56.335 align:start size:63% position:38%
And finally,
the human blink--
specifically, yours.

00:08:56.368 --> 00:08:58.837 align:start position:0% line:0%
You can tell it's mine
because of the rogue brow.

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:00.673 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- Gav:<i> Look at that. </i>
-<i> Yeah.</i>

00:09:00.706 --> 00:09:02.641 line:0%
Gav:<i> Filmed at
 1,000 frames a second,</i>

00:09:02.674 --> 00:09:07.012
if shot at 100 billion,
it would take 19 years

00:09:07.045 --> 00:09:08.847 line:0%
<i> for you to close
 and open your eyes.</i>

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:10.316 line:0%
<i> ( Dan gasps )</i>

00:09:10.349 --> 00:09:11.917 line:0%
Gav:<i> The things that you
 wouldn't have seen.</i>

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:13.285 align:start position:0%
It would be like
I'd be just about

00:09:13.318 --> 00:09:16.021 align:start position:0%
to celebrate the millennium,
and then I'd be like,

00:09:16.054 --> 00:09:17.022 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Whoa!"

00:09:17.055 --> 00:09:19.892 align:start size:50% position:50%
"3, 2, 1! 2019?"

00:09:19.925 --> 00:09:23.195 align:start size:88% position:13%
You blinked
and missed the millennium.

00:09:23.228 --> 00:09:25.197 line:0%
<i> And absurdly,
 if we filmed this</i>

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:26.999 line:0%
<i> at 10 trillion frames
 a second,</i>

00:09:27.032 --> 00:09:29.702
the most boring video ever,

00:09:29.735 --> 00:09:33.872
it would take 1,900 years

00:09:33.905 --> 00:09:35.007 align:start size:88% position:13%
for you to open
your eyes again.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.209 size:84% position:42%
So, I'd just start blinking

00:09:37.242 --> 00:09:39.211
when Jesus was cutting around.

00:09:39.244 --> 00:09:41.046 align:start position:0%
I'd open them up
and it would be World War I.

00:09:41.079 --> 00:09:42.314 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Pretty much.
- Right.

00:09:42.347 --> 00:09:44.183 align:start size:56% position:44%
That's a great way
of imagining that.

00:09:44.216 --> 00:09:46.785 align:start size:88% position:13%
And just think,
in all that time,

00:09:46.818 --> 00:09:48.721 align:start size:88% position:13%
a few photons of light
would just be like,

00:09:48.754 --> 00:09:51.256 size:88% position:44%
"Ah, there we go,"
like it was nothing.

00:09:51.289 --> 00:09:55.060 align:start position:0%
That was honestly
an absurd episode.

00:09:55.093 --> 00:09:57.363 align:start position:0%
To take something that--
you know, everyday time,
we're used to it,

00:09:57.396 --> 00:10:00.733 align:start position:0%
but to take it
to that extreme level,

00:10:00.766 --> 00:10:02.735 align:start position:0%
you could barely
even get your head around.
I quite liked it.

00:10:02.768 --> 00:10:04.236 align:start position:0%
I liked how much
it messed with my head.

00:10:04.269 --> 00:10:06.238 align:start size:63% position:38%
Me, too.
I quite liked how
they were telling us

00:10:06.271 --> 00:10:07.373 align:start size:63% position:38%
about the real world
applications

00:10:07.406 --> 00:10:08.741 align:start size:63% position:38%
it could be used for
in the future,

00:10:08.774 --> 00:10:10.075 align:start size:63% position:38%
like the police
might want to use it

00:10:10.108 --> 00:10:12.344 align:start size:66% position:34%
to see around corners
with that lidar.

00:10:12.377 --> 00:10:14.747 align:start size:63% position:38%
Like the sonar where
they fire light out

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:16.048 align:start size:50% position:50%
and bounces off
all the surfaces

00:10:16.081 --> 00:10:17.049 align:start size:59% position:41%
and they see people
around the corner.

00:10:17.082 --> 00:10:18.250 size:81% position:41%
Yeah, or easily being able

00:10:18.283 --> 00:10:19.785 align:start position:0%
to see through skin
with one picture.

00:10:19.818 --> 00:10:21.420 align:start size:69% position:31%
Yeah, you can see
how a wound is healing

00:10:21.453 --> 00:10:23.756 size:84% position:58%
or you can basically take
a MRI with your cell phone.

00:10:23.789 --> 00:10:25.090 align:start position:0%
It makes me
slightly terrified

00:10:25.123 --> 00:10:27.760 align:start position:0%
about the future
of surveillance technology,

00:10:27.793 --> 00:10:30.262 align:start position:0%
but, you know,
we'll see what happens.

00:10:30.295 --> 00:10:31.764
Hopefully,
you enjoyed that video.

00:10:31.797 --> 00:10:33.098
Make sure you check out
other episodes

00:10:33.131 --> 00:10:34.099
from "Planet Slow Mo."

00:10:34.132 --> 00:10:35.768
and you can subscribe to us,

00:10:35.801 --> 00:10:37.269
the Slow Mo Guys,
if you want.

00:10:37.302 --> 00:10:40.773
The episodes aren't usually
that heavy, I promise.

00:10:40.806 --> 00:10:42.174 align:start size:75% position:25%
Those guys were so smart
and they wore lab coats.

00:10:42.207 --> 00:10:43.742 align:start size:75% position:25%
I feel like
I should just be, like--

00:10:43.775 --> 00:10:45.211 align:start size:91% position:9%
- We should take ours off.
- Just take it.

